{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 5768 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "8217a89d1d8eaa8f48eea2b32ed047ee9a481b20494953461040cc9875ef843c" :edb/db-before-t 5767 :edb/db-after-t 5768 :edb/tx-data [[13194139539080 50 #inst "2026-10-23T17:58:30.979Z" 13194139539080 17592186576411 true] [17592186083865 1071 "Calculate\n$$\n\\displaystyle ∑_(n = 1)^(50)(2n^{2} + 3n).\n$$\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139539080 17592186576411 true] [17592186083865 1071 "Calculate\n$∑_(n = 1)^(50)(2n^{2} + 3n)$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139539080 17592186576411 false] [17592186083867 1071 "$$\n\\displaystyle ∑_(n = 1)^(25)n^{3} =\n$$\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139539080 17592186576411 true] [17592186083867 1071 "$∑_(n = 1)^(25)n^{3} =$\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139539080 17592186576411 false] [17592186083868 1071 "Calculate\n$$\n\\displaystyle ∑_(n = 1)^(25)4n^{3}.\n$$\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139539080 17592186576411 true] [17592186083868 1071 "Calculate\n$∑_(n = 1)^(25)4n^{3}$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139539080 17592186576411 false] [17592186083872 1071 "Calculate the period of the function $$\n\\displaystyle y=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(3x+\\frac{\\pi }{4})-1.\n$$" 13194139539080 17592186576411 true] [17592186083872 1071 "Calculate the period of the function $y=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(3x+\\frac{\\pi }{4})-1.$" 13194139539080 17592186576411 false] [17592186083874 1071 "Find the range of the function $$\n\\displaystyle f(x)=\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{8})-1.\n$$" 13194139539080 17592186576411 true] [17592186083874 1071 "Find the range of the function $f(x)=\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{8})-1.$" 13194139539080 17592186576411 false] [17592186083874 1074 "The range of $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ consists of two intervals:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty )\n$$\n\nHorizontal shifts and stretches have no effect on the range. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nWe can split this into two inequalities:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\le -1,\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\ge 1\n$$\n\nTo find the range of $f(x),$ we consider both intervals separately.\n\n- Multiplying the first interval by $\\frac{1}{3}$ and subtracting $1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\le -1 \\\\  & \\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\le -\\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & \\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{8})-1\\le -\\frac{1}{3}-1 \\\\  & f(x)\\le -\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Multiplying the second interval by $\\frac{1}{3}$ and subtracting $1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\ge 1 \\\\  & \\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\ge \\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & \\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{8})-1\\ge \\frac{1}{3}-1 \\\\  & f(x)\\ge -\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCombining both inequalities, we get\n\n$$\nf(x)\\le -\\frac{4}{3},\\,f(x)\\ge -\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the range is $$\n\\displaystyle f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,-\\frac{4}{3}]\\cup [-\\frac{2}{3},\\infty ).\n$$" 13194139539080 17592186576411 true] [17592186083874 1074 "The range of $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ consists of two intervals:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty )\n$$\n\nHorizontal shifts and stretches have no effect on the range. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nWe can split this into two inequalities:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\le -1,\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\ge 1\n$$\n\nTo find the range of $f(x),$ we consider both intervals separately.\n\n- Multiplying the first interval by $\\frac{1}{3}$ and subtracting $1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\le -1 \\\\  & \\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\le -\\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & \\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{8})-1\\le -\\frac{1}{3}-1 \\\\  & f(x)\\le -\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Multiplying the second interval by $\\frac{1}{3}$ and subtracting $1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\ge 1 \\\\  & \\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\ge \\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & \\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{8})-1\\ge \\frac{1}{3}-1 \\\\  & f(x)\\ge -\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCombining both inequalities, we get\n\n$$\nf(x)\\le -\\frac{4}{3},\\,f(x)\\ge -\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the range is $f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,-\\frac{4}{3}]\\cup [-\\frac{2}{3},\\infty ).$" 13194139539080 17592186576411 false] [17592186083875 1071 "What is the value of $$\n\\displaystyle f(x)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(3x+\\frac{\\pi }{4})+2\n$$ at its local maximum points?" 13194139539080 17592186576411 true] [17592186083875 1071 "What is the value of $f(x)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(3x+\\frac{\\pi }{4})+2$ at its local maximum points?" 13194139539080 17592186576411 false] [17592186083876 1071 "What is the value of $$\n\\displaystyle f(x) = 2\\csc (\\frac{x}{3} - \\frac{π}{6}) + 3\n$$ at its local minimum points?" 13194139539080 17592186576411 true] [17592186083876 1071 "What is the value of $f(x) = 2\\csc (\\frac{x}{3} - \\frac{π}{6}) + 3$ at its local minimum points?" 13194139539080 17592186576411 false] [17592186083896 1071 "$$\n\\displaystyle ∑_(n = 1)^(120)\\frac{n}{2} =\n$$\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139539080 17592186576411 true] [17592186083896 1071 "$∑_(n = 1)^(120)\\frac{n}{2} =$\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139539080 17592186576411 false] [17592186083897 1071 "Calculate\n$$\n\\displaystyle ∑_(n = 1)^(90)(8n - \\frac{1}{4}).\n$$\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139539080 17592186576411 true] [17592186083897 1071 "Calculate\n$∑_(n = 1)^(90)(8n - \\frac{1}{4})$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139539080 17592186576411 false] [17592186083898 1071 "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle ∑_(n = 1)^(60)\\frac{n^{2}}{11}.\n$$\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139539080 17592186576411 true] [17592186083898 1071 "Calculate $∑_(n = 1)^(60)\\frac{n^{2}}{11}$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139539080 17592186576411 false] [17592186083900 1071 "Evaluate the series $$\n\\displaystyle ∑_(n = 1)^(6)16\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^{n - 1}.\n$$\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139539080 17592186576411 true] [17592186083900 1071 "Evaluate the series $∑_(n = 1)^(6)16\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^{n - 1}$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139539080 17592186576411 false] [17592186083901 1071 "Evaluate the series $$\n\\displaystyle ∑_(n = 1)^(4)9\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)^{n}.\n$$\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139539080 17592186576411 true] [17592186083901 1071 "Evaluate the series $∑_(n = 1)^(4)9\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)^{n}$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139539080 17592186576411 false] [17592186083902 1071 "Evaluate the geometric series $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{3}\\frac{1}{{5}^{n-1}}.\n$$" 13194139539080 17592186576411 true] [17592186083902 1071 "Evaluate the geometric series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{3}\\frac{1}{{5}^{n-1}}.$" 13194139539080 17592186576411 false] [17592186083902 1074 "Notice that we can rewrite the series as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{3}\\frac{1}{{5}^{n-1}}={\\sum }_{n=1}^{3}{\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{n-1}\n$$\n\nSince the above series is in the form $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}{a}_{1}{r}^{n-1},\n$$ it is a geometric series.\n\nTo determine the sum, we use the formula for the sum ${S}_{N}$ of a geometric series.\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}={a}_{1}\\frac{(1-{r}^{N})}{1-r}\n$$\n\nIn this case, ${a}_{1}=1,$ $r=\\frac{1}{5}$ and $N=3.$ If we substitute these values into the above formula, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{3}\\frac{1}{{5}^{n-1}} & =1\\cdot \\frac{[1-{\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{3}]}{(1-\\frac{1}{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{124}{125}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{31}{25}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139539080 17592186576411 true] [17592186083902 1074 "Notice that we can rewrite the series as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{3}\\frac{1}{{5}^{n-1}}={\\sum }_{n=1}^{3}{\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{n-1}\n$$\n\nSince the above series is in the form ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}{a}_{1}{r}^{n-1},$ it is a geometric series.\n\nTo determine the sum, we use the formula for the sum ${S}_{N}$ of a geometric series.\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}={a}_{1}\\frac{(1-{r}^{N})}{1-r}\n$$\n\nIn this case, ${a}_{1}=1,$ $r=\\frac{1}{5}$ and $N=3.$ If we substitute these values into the above formula, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{3}\\frac{1}{{5}^{n-1}} & =1\\cdot \\frac{[1-{\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{3}]}{(1-\\frac{1}{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{124}{125}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{31}{25}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139539080 17592186576411 false] [17592186083903 1071 "Determine the value of the series $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}\\frac{1}{{6}^{n}}\n$$  rounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139539080 17592186576411 true] [17592186083903 1071 "Determine the value of the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}\\frac{1}{{6}^{n}}$  rounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139539080 17592186576411 false] [17592186083903 1074 "Notice that we can rewrite the series as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}\\frac{1}{{6}^{n}}={\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}{\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)}^{n}\n$$\n\nSince the above series is in the form $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}a{r}^{n},\n$$ it is a geometric series. \n\nTo determine the sum, we use the formula for the sum ${S}_{N}$ of a geometric series.\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}={a}_{1}\\frac{(1-{r}^{N})}{1-r}\n$$\n\nIn this case, $r=\\frac{1}{6}$ and $N=4.$ We need to figure out ${a}_{1}.$\n\nThe formula for the $n$th term of the series is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)}^{n},\n$$\n\nand therefore the first term of the series is\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}={\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)}^{1}=\\frac{1}{6}.\n$$\n\nIf we substitute ${a}_{1}=\\frac{1}{6},r=\\frac{1}{6}$ and $N=4$ into the formula for ${S}_{N}$, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}{\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)}^{n} & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot \\frac{[1-{\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)}^{4}]}{(1-\\frac{1}{6})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot [1-{\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)}^{4}] \\\\  & ≈0.200\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the final answer has been rounded to three decimal places." 13194139539080 17592186576411 true] [17592186083903 1074 "Notice that we can rewrite the series as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}\\frac{1}{{6}^{n}}={\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}{\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)}^{n}\n$$\n\nSince the above series is in the form ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}a{r}^{n},$ it is a geometric series. \n\nTo determine the sum, we use the formula for the sum ${S}_{N}$ of a geometric series.\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}={a}_{1}\\frac{(1-{r}^{N})}{1-r}\n$$\n\nIn this case, $r=\\frac{1}{6}$ and $N=4.$ We need to figure out ${a}_{1}.$\n\nThe formula for the $n$th term of the series is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)}^{n},\n$$\n\nand therefore the first term of the series is\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}={\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)}^{1}=\\frac{1}{6}.\n$$\n\nIf we substitute ${a}_{1}=\\frac{1}{6},r=\\frac{1}{6}$ and $N=4$ into the formula for ${S}_{N}$, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}{\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)}^{n} & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot \\frac{[1-{\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)}^{4}]}{(1-\\frac{1}{6})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot [1-{\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)}^{4}] \\\\  & ≈0.200\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the final answer has been rounded to three decimal places." 13194139539080 17592186576411 false] [17592186083904 1071 "Evaluate the series $$\n\\displaystyle ∑_(n = 1)^(5)\\frac{64}{2^{n}}.\n$$\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139539080 17592186576411 true] [17592186083904 1071 "Evaluate the series $∑_(n = 1)^(5)\\frac{64}{2^{n}}$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139539080 17592186576411 false] [17592186083905 1071 "Determine the value of the geometric series $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=0}^{7}8{\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)}^{n-1}\n$$ rounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139539080 17592186576411 true] [17592186083905 1071 "Determine the value of the geometric series ${\\sum }_{n=0}^{7}8{\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)}^{n-1}$ rounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139539080 17592186576411 false] [17592186083906 1071 "Calculate\n$$\n\\displaystyle ∑_(n = 1)^(30)(2 - 6n^{3}).\n$$\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139539080 17592186576411 true] [17592186083906 1071 "Calculate\n$∑_(n = 1)^(30)(2 - 6n^{3})$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139539080 17592186576411 false] [17592186083913 1071 "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n$$\n\\displaystyle x = t^{2} - 2t, y = t^{5}, - ∞ < t < ∞.\n$$\n\nCalculate the equation of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t =-1$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139539080 17592186576411 true] [17592186083913 1071 "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n$x = t^{2} - 2t, y = t^{5}, - ∞ < t < ∞$.\n\nCalculate the equation of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t =-1$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139539080 17592186576411 false] [17592186083918 1071 "Calculate\n$$\n\\displaystyle ∑_(n = 1)^(30)\\frac{1}{3}n^{3}.\n$$\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139539080 17592186576411 true] [17592186083918 1071 "Calculate\n$∑_(n = 1)^(30)\\frac{1}{3}n^{3}$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139539080 17592186576411 false] [17592186083922 1074 "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{4}-3{t}^{3}+t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}{t}^{5}-\\frac{3}{4}{t}^{4}+\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(0)=-10.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-10 & =\\frac{1}{10}(0)^{5}-\\frac{3}{4}(0)^{4}+\\frac{1}{2}(0)^{2}+C \\\\ -10 & =0+C \\\\ C & =-10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $$\n\\displaystyle v(t)=\\frac{1}{10}{t}^{5}-\\frac{3}{4}{t}^{4}+\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}-10.\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the velocity at time $t=2\\,\\text{s}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(2) & =\\frac{1}{10}(2)^{5}-\\frac{3}{4}(2)^{4}+\\frac{1}{2}(2)^{2}-10 \\\\  & =3.2-12+2-10 \\\\  & =-16.8\\,\\text{m/s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the speed is equal to $|v(t)|,$ we conclude that the speed of the particle is $16.8\\,\\text{m/s}.$" 13194139539080 17592186576411 true] [17592186083922 1074 "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{4}-3{t}^{3}+t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}{t}^{5}-\\frac{3}{4}{t}^{4}+\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(0)=-10.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-10 & =\\frac{1}{10}(0)^{5}-\\frac{3}{4}(0)^{4}+\\frac{1}{2}(0)^{2}+C \\\\ -10 & =0+C \\\\ C & =-10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=\\frac{1}{10}{t}^{5}-\\frac{3}{4}{t}^{4}+\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}-10.$\n\nWe evaluate the velocity at time $t=2\\,\\text{s}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(2) & =\\frac{1}{10}(2)^{5}-\\frac{3}{4}(2)^{4}+\\frac{1}{2}(2)^{2}-10 \\\\  & =3.2-12+2-10 \\\\  & =-16.8\\,\\text{m/s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the speed is equal to $|v(t)|,$ we conclude that the speed of the particle is $16.8\\,\\text{m/s}.$" 13194139539080 17592186576411 false] [17592186083927 1071 "Find the solution $(x, y)$ to the following system of equations using the elimination method:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}2 x + 3 y = 13 \\\\ x - 3 y = - 7\\end{array} \\right.\n$$\n\n*Hint: Eliminate the variable $y$.*" 13194139539080 17592186576411 true] [17592186083927 1071 "Find the solution $(x, y)$ to the following system of equations using the elimination method:\n\n$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}2 x + 3 y = 13 \\\\ x - 3 y = - 7\\end{array} \\right.$\n\n*Hint: Eliminate the variable $y$.*" 13194139539080 17592186576411 false] [17592186083931 1071 "What is the center of the hyperbola $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{16}-\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{4}=1?\n$$" 13194139539080 17592186576411 true] [17592186083931 1071 "What is the center of the hyperbola $\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{16}-\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{4}=1?$" 13194139539080 17592186576411 false] [17592186083932 1071 "What is the center of the hyperbola $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{7}-\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{6}=1?\n$$" 13194139539080 17592186576411 true] [17592186083932 1071 "What is the center of the hyperbola $\\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{7}-\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{6}=1?$" 13194139539080 17592186576411 false] [17592186083933 1071 "What is the center of the hyperbola $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{16}-\\frac{(x-5)^{2}}{4}=1?\n$$" 13194139539080 17592186576411 true] [17592186083933 1071 "What is the center of the hyperbola $\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{16}-\\frac{(x-5)^{2}}{4}=1?$" 13194139539080 17592186576411 false] [17592186083938 1071 "The length of a curve $y=f(x)$ is given by $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{2+4{x}^{2}-4x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$ Which of the following could be  an equation for this curve?" 13194139539080 17592186576411 true] [17592186083938 1071 "The length of a curve $y=f(x)$ is given by ${\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{2+4{x}^{2}-4x}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Which of the following could be  an equation for this curve?" 13194139539080 17592186576411 false] [17592186083939 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to \n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-6}^{-3}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-9}}?\n$$" 13194139539080 17592186576411 true] [17592186083939 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to \n${\\int }_{-6}^{-3}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-9}}?$" 13194139539080 17592186576411 false] [17592186083940 1071 "Consider the series $$\n\\displaystyle S = ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)\\frac{4}{2n + 1}.\n$$ Which of the following is true?" 13194139539080 17592186576411 true] [17592186083940 1071 "Consider the series $S = ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)\\frac{4}{2n + 1}$. Which of the following is true?" 13194139539080 17592186576411 false] [17592186083941 1071 "Given the series $$\n\\displaystyle S = ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)\\frac{n + 1}{n^{2}},\n$$ which of the following is true?" 13194139539080 17592186576411 true] [17592186083941 1071 "Given the series $S = ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)\\frac{n + 1}{n^{2}}$, which of the following is true?" 13194139539080 17592186576411 false] [17592186083942 1071 "Let $a_{n} = (3(-1)^{n})/(\\sqrt{n + 2})$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $$\n\\displaystyle ∑_(n = 1)^(∞) \\mid a_{n} \\mid\n$$ is convergent\n2. $∑_(n = 1)^(∞)a_{n}$ is convergent\n3. The terms of the series $∑_(n = 1)^(∞)a_{n}$ can be rearranged so that the new series converges to $\\sqrt{3}$" 13194139539080 17592186576411 true] [17592186083942 1071 "Let $a_{n} = (3(-1)^{n})/(\\sqrt{n + 2})$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $∑_(n = 1)^(∞) \\mid a_{n} \\mid$ is convergent\n2. $∑_(n = 1)^(∞)a_{n}$ is convergent\n3. The terms of the series $∑_(n = 1)^(∞)a_{n}$ can be rearranged so that the new series converges to $\\sqrt{3}$" 13194139539080 17592186576411 false] [17592186083987 1071 "Evaluate the series $$\n\\displaystyle ∑_(n = 1)^(7)\\frac{1}{2}(3)^{n}.\n$$\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139539080 17592186576411 true] [17592186083987 1071 "Evaluate the series $∑_(n = 1)^(7)\\frac{1}{2}(3)^{n}$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139539080 17592186576411 false] [17592186083989 1071 "Solve the equation $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{S+1}+\\frac{2}{S-1}=\\frac{1}{{S}^{2}-1}.\n$$" 13194139539080 17592186576411 true] [17592186083989 1071 "Solve the equation $\\frac{1}{S+1}+\\frac{2}{S-1}=\\frac{1}{{S}^{2}-1}.$" 13194139539080 17592186576411 false] [17592186083990 1071 "Given that\n$$\n\\displaystyle f(0) = 3, f(3) = 6, f(6) = 0,\n$$\n\nfind the value of $f^{-1}(3)$." 13194139539080 17592186576411 true] [17592186083990 1071 "Given that\n$f(0) = 3, f(3) = 6, f(6) = 0$,\n\nfind the value of $f^{-1}(3)$." 13194139539080 17592186576411 false] [17592186084398 1064 "The **$n$th term test for divergence** is a method that can be used to show that an infinite series diverges.\n\nSuppose we have an infinite series\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term test for divergence states that\n\n- If $\\displaystyle \\lim_{n\\to\\infty} a_n \\neq 0$, then the series is divergent.\n- If $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{n\\to\\infty} a_n = 0,\n$$ then the $n$th term test gives *no conclusion* regarding the convergence or divergence of the series.\n\nThe $n$th term test can *only* be used to show that a series diverges. It *cannot* be used to show that a series converges.\n\n**Note:** The $n$th term for divergence is simultaneously intuitive and counterintuitive.\n\n- The first part of the $n$th term test is intuitive. If the limit of the terms of the series is not $0$, then the partial sums of the series are always changing by a significant amount, which means they never \"settle\" to a single value.\n- The second part of the $n$th term test may seem counterintuitive. If the limit of the terms of the series is $0$, it's tempting to think that the series must converge. However, as we will see in the future, this is not the case. There are many series that diverge despite the limit of their terms being $0$." 13194139539080 17592186576411 true] [17592186084398 1064 "The **$n$th term test for divergence** is a method that can be used to show that an infinite series diverges.\n\nSuppose we have an infinite series\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term test for divergence states that\n\n- If $\\displaystyle \\lim_{n\\to\\infty} a_n \\neq 0$, then the series is divergent.\n- If $\\displaystyle \\lim_{n\\to\\infty} a_n = 0$, then the $n$th term test gives *no conclusion* regarding the convergence or divergence of the series.\n\nThe $n$th term test can *only* be used to show that a series diverges. It *cannot* be used to show that a series converges.\n\n**Note:** The $n$th term for divergence is simultaneously intuitive and counterintuitive.\n\n- The first part of the $n$th term test is intuitive. If the limit of the terms of the series is not $0$, then the partial sums of the series are always changing by a significant amount, which means they never \"settle\" to a single value.\n- The second part of the $n$th term test may seem counterintuitive. If the limit of the terms of the series is $0$, it's tempting to think that the series must converge. However, as we will see in the future, this is not the case. There are many series that diverge despite the limit of their terms being $0$." 13194139539080 17592186576411 false] [17592186084400 1064 "The **integral test** is used to determine whether an infinite series\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n}\n$$\n\nconverges or diverges. It states the following:\n\n> *If $f(x)$ is a positive, continuous, and decreasing function for $x\\geq 1$ such that $f(n)=a_n$, and the integral*\n> $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_1^\\infty f(x) \\: \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n> *is convergent, then the series*\n> $\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n$\n> *is also convergent. The opposite is also true, i.e., if the integral is divergent, then the series is also divergent.*\n\nFor example, suppose that we want to determine the convergence of the series\n\n$$\n\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac 1 {n^{3/2}}\n$$\n\nWe can compare it to the integral\n\n$$\n\\int_1^\\infty \\dfrac {1} {x^{3/2}} \\: \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nHere,\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x^{3/2}}\n$$\n\nis a positive, continuous, and decreasing function for\n\n$$\nx\\geq1\n$$\n\nso the integral test applies. Let's compute the integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{1}^{∞}\\frac{1}{x^{3/2}}dx &= lim_(b → ∞)\\int_{1}^{b}\\frac{1}{x^{3/2}}dx \\\\\n&= lim_(b → ∞)[- 2\\left(\\frac{1}{x^{1/2}}\\right)]_{1}^{b} \\\\\n&=-2lim_(b → ∞)[\\frac{1}{b^{1/2}} - \\frac{1}{1^{1/2}}] \\\\\n&=-2[0 - 1] \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the integral is convergent, the integral test guarantees that the series\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac 1 {n^{3/2}}\n$$\n\nis also convergent." 13194139539080 17592186576411 true] [17592186084400 1064 "The **integral test** is used to determine whether an infinite series\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n}\n$$\n\nconverges or diverges. It states the following:\n\n> *If $f(x)$ is a positive, continuous, and decreasing function for $x\\geq 1$ such that $f(n)=a_n$, and the integral*\n> $\\displaystyle \\int_1^\\infty f(x) \\: \\textrm{d}x$\n> *is convergent, then the series*\n> $\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n$\n> *is also convergent. The opposite is also true, i.e., if the integral is divergent, then the series is also divergent.*\n\nFor example, suppose that we want to determine the convergence of the series\n\n$$\n\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac 1 {n^{3/2}}\n$$\n\nWe can compare it to the integral\n\n$$\n\\int_1^\\infty \\dfrac {1} {x^{3/2}} \\: \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nHere,\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x^{3/2}}\n$$\n\nis a positive, continuous, and decreasing function for\n\n$$\nx\\geq1\n$$\n\nso the integral test applies. Let's compute the integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{1}^{∞}\\frac{1}{x^{3/2}}dx &= lim_(b → ∞)\\int_{1}^{b}\\frac{1}{x^{3/2}}dx \\\\\n&= lim_(b → ∞)[- 2\\left(\\frac{1}{x^{1/2}}\\right)]_{1}^{b} \\\\\n&=-2lim_(b → ∞)[\\frac{1}{b^{1/2}} - \\frac{1}{1^{1/2}}] \\\\\n&=-2[0 - 1] \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the integral is convergent, the integral test guarantees that the series\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac 1 {n^{3/2}}\n$$\n\nis also convergent." 13194139539080 17592186576411 false] [17592186084402 1064 "We've gotten some practice using the integral test to determine whether a series converges or diverges. But why does the integral test work?\n\nLet's start off by visualizing a positive, continuous, and decreasing function $f(x)$ on a graph. Let's also visualize the series $a_n$ such that\n\n$$\nf(n)=a_n\n$$\n\n![](images/6a/6a3e4e526a1efd67a4260bfe703d5c4edc18a4736828e244e134451e51ef190d.png)\n\nThe integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_1^\\infty f(x) \\: \\textrm dx\n$$ represents the area under the curve, while the series\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n\n$$\n\nrepresents the area of all the rectangles.\n\nBased on the plot above, we see that for $x>1$, the combined area of all the rectangles is greater than the area under the curve. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\int_{1}^{∞}f(x)dx & < a_{1} + a_{2} + a_{3} + … \\\\ \\int_{1}^{∞}f(x)dx & < ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)a_{n}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis means that if $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_1^\\infty f(x)\n$$ is divergent, then so is\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n\n$$\n\nNotice that we can also draw the rectangles a different way, as shown below.\n\n![](images/10/109861c3df0a25bf8681d25dca8ef55a871960fc1bbb6db936be9d6d603f6a15.png)\n\nBased on the plot above, we see that for $x>1$, the combined area of all the rectangles is less than the area under the curve. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}a_{2} + a_{3} + a_{4} + … & < \\int_{1}^{∞}f(x)dx \\\\ ∑_(n = 2)^(∞)a_{n} & < \\int_{1}^{∞}f(x)dx\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis means that if $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_1^\\infty f(x)\n$$ is convergent, then so is\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=2}^\\infty a_n\n$$\n\nMoreover, since $a_1$ is a finite number and\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n = a_1 + \\displaystyle \\sum_{n=2}^\\infty a_n\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n\n$$\n\nmust also be convergent." 13194139539080 17592186576411 true] [17592186084402 1064 "We've gotten some practice using the integral test to determine whether a series converges or diverges. But why does the integral test work?\n\nLet's start off by visualizing a positive, continuous, and decreasing function $f(x)$ on a graph. Let's also visualize the series $a_n$ such that\n\n$$\nf(n)=a_n\n$$\n\n![](images/6a/6a3e4e526a1efd67a4260bfe703d5c4edc18a4736828e244e134451e51ef190d.png)\n\nThe integral $\\displaystyle \\int_1^\\infty f(x) \\: \\textrm dx$ represents the area under the curve, while the series\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n\n$$\n\nrepresents the area of all the rectangles.\n\nBased on the plot above, we see that for $x>1$, the combined area of all the rectangles is greater than the area under the curve. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\int_{1}^{∞}f(x)dx & < a_{1} + a_{2} + a_{3} + … \\\\ \\int_{1}^{∞}f(x)dx & < ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)a_{n}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis means that if $\\displaystyle \\int_1^\\infty f(x)$ is divergent, then so is\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n\n$$\n\nNotice that we can also draw the rectangles a different way, as shown below.\n\n![](images/10/109861c3df0a25bf8681d25dca8ef55a871960fc1bbb6db936be9d6d603f6a15.png)\n\nBased on the plot above, we see that for $x>1$, the combined area of all the rectangles is less than the area under the curve. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}a_{2} + a_{3} + a_{4} + … & < \\int_{1}^{∞}f(x)dx \\\\ ∑_(n = 2)^(∞)a_{n} & < \\int_{1}^{∞}f(x)dx\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis means that if $\\displaystyle \\int_1^\\infty f(x)$ is convergent, then so is\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=2}^\\infty a_n\n$$\n\nMoreover, since $a_1$ is a finite number and\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n = a_1 + \\displaystyle \\sum_{n=2}^\\infty a_n\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n\n$$\n\nmust also be convergent." 13194139539080 17592186576411 false] [17592186084403 1064 "The **comparison test for convergence** is a method that we can use to show that a series is convergent. The idea is to show that the series is *less than or equal to* some other series that is known to be convergent.\n\nMore precisely, the comparison test for convergence states the following:\n\n> *Consider two series $$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty b_n\n$$ such that $a_n$ and $b_n$ are both positive and $a_n \\leq b_n$ for all $n$. If $$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty b_n\n$$ is convergent, then $$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n\n$$ is also convergent.*\n\nFor example, let's apply the comparison test to show that the series\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{n^2+1}\n$$\n\nis convergent. We will compare the series above to the series\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{n^2}\n$$\n\nIndeed, both\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{n^2+1}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{n^2}\n$$\n\nare positive, and\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{n^2+1} < \\dfrac{1}{n^2}\n$$\n\nfor all\n\n$$\nn \\geq 1\n$$\n\nIn particular, the above inequality is true because the denominator on the left-hand side is larger than the denominator on the right-hand side, which means the fraction on the left-hand side is smaller.\n\nWe know that\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{n^2}\n$$\n\nis convergent because it is a $p$-series with $p =2 > 1$. Therefore, by the comparison test, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{n^2+1}\n$$\n\nis also convergent." 13194139539080 17592186576411 true] [17592186084403 1064 "The **comparison test for convergence** is a method that we can use to show that a series is convergent. The idea is to show that the series is *less than or equal to* some other series that is known to be convergent.\n\nMore precisely, the comparison test for convergence states the following:\n\n> *Consider two series $\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n$ and $\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty b_n$ such that $a_n$ and $b_n$ are both positive and $a_n \\leq b_n$ for all $n$. If $\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty b_n$ is convergent, then $\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n$ is also convergent.*\n\nFor example, let's apply the comparison test to show that the series\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{n^2+1}\n$$\n\nis convergent. We will compare the series above to the series\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{n^2}\n$$\n\nIndeed, both\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{n^2+1}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{n^2}\n$$\n\nare positive, and\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{n^2+1} < \\dfrac{1}{n^2}\n$$\n\nfor all\n\n$$\nn \\geq 1\n$$\n\nIn particular, the above inequality is true because the denominator on the left-hand side is larger than the denominator on the right-hand side, which means the fraction on the left-hand side is smaller.\n\nWe know that\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{n^2}\n$$\n\nis convergent because it is a $p$-series with $p =2 > 1$. Therefore, by the comparison test, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{n^2+1}\n$$\n\nis also convergent." 13194139539080 17592186576411 false] [17592186084404 1064 "We can use the **comparison test for divergence** to check if a series is divergent. The idea is to show that the series is *greater than or equal to* some other series that is known to be divergent.\n\nMore precisely, the comparison test for divergence states the following:\n\n> *Consider two series $$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty b_n\n$$ such that $a_n$ and $b_n$ are both positive and $a_n \\geq b_n$ for all $n$. If $$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty b_n\n$$ is divergent, then $$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n\n$$ is also divergent.*\n\nFor example, let's apply the comparison test to show that the series\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{2n-1}\n$$\n\nis divergent. We will compare the series above to the series\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{2n}\n$$\n\nIndeed, both\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2n-1}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2n}\n$$\n\nare positive, and\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2n-1} \\geq \\dfrac{1}{2n}\n$$\n\nfor all\n\n$$\nn \\geq 1\n$$\n\nThe above inequality is true because the denominator on the left-hand side is smaller than the denominator on the right-hand side, which means the fraction on the left-hand side is larger.\n\nWe know that\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{2n}\n$$\n\nis divergent because it is a $p$-series with\n\n$$\np \\leq 1\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the comparison test, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{2n-1}\n$$\n\nis also divergent." 13194139539080 17592186576411 true] [17592186084404 1064 "We can use the **comparison test for divergence** to check if a series is divergent. The idea is to show that the series is *greater than or equal to* some other series that is known to be divergent.\n\nMore precisely, the comparison test for divergence states the following:\n\n> *Consider two series $\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n$ and $\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty b_n$ such that $a_n$ and $b_n$ are both positive and $a_n \\geq b_n$ for all $n$. If $\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty b_n$ is divergent, then $\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n$ is also divergent.*\n\nFor example, let's apply the comparison test to show that the series\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{2n-1}\n$$\n\nis divergent. We will compare the series above to the series\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{2n}\n$$\n\nIndeed, both\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2n-1}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2n}\n$$\n\nare positive, and\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2n-1} \\geq \\dfrac{1}{2n}\n$$\n\nfor all\n\n$$\nn \\geq 1\n$$\n\nThe above inequality is true because the denominator on the left-hand side is smaller than the denominator on the right-hand side, which means the fraction on the left-hand side is larger.\n\nWe know that\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{2n}\n$$\n\nis divergent because it is a $p$-series with\n\n$$\np \\leq 1\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the comparison test, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{2n-1}\n$$\n\nis also divergent." 13194139539080 17592186576411 false] [17592186084418 1064 "When we multiply the argument of $y=\\sin x$ by some number $B,$ the resulting function $y=\\sin(Bx)$ is a horizontal scaling of the original graph by a scaling factor $\\dfrac{1}{B}.$ More specifically, it is said to be\n\n- a **horizontal stretch** if $\\dfrac{1}{B} > 1,$ or\n\n- a **horizontal shrink** if $0 < \\dfrac{1}{B} < 1.$\n\nIn general, the period of $y=\\sin (Bx)$ is equal to the period of $y=\\sin x$ (which is $2\\pi$) multiplied by the scaling factor:\n\n$$\n\\text{period} = 2\\pi\\cdot \\dfrac{1}{B}\n$$\n\nFor example, consider the graph of $\\displaystyle y=\\sin{2x}$ shown below. For this function, we have $B=2,$ so the 