eriod $\\pi \\cdot 2 = 2\\pi.$\n\n![](images/73/73b19f8883bc061da7e9b0f5823c547a6cb6932af863a39d7aa59ec4031ea033.png)" 13194139539080 17592186576411 false] [17592186084427 1064 "The secant and cosecant functions have a local minimum on each of the positive branches when the function value is equal to $1,$ as shown below:\n\n![](images/35/35f69692440cd1eb636c591356b0a73d91d30503968764b45e62fe66222e5da9.png)\n\nSimilarly, the functions have a local maximum on each of the negative branches when the function value is equal to $-1\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/58/58744883b5f83ce11d5a3da8d2deda8840020bd903130fa464bfa2f31627e436.png)\n\nHowever, the values at the local minimum and maximum points of a transformed secant or cosecant function change according to the applied transformations.\n\nFor instance, let's find the value of $$\n\\displaystyle f(x)={\\color{red}2}\\sec\\left( {\\color{blue}3}x + \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)+{\\color{green}1}\n$$ at its local minimum points.\n\nOn a positive branch of the graph of $y=\\sec x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\sec x \\geq 1.\n$$\n\nThe points where $\\sec x = 1$ are local minima of the graph of $y=\\sec x.$ \n\nHorizontal shifts and stretches have no effect on the range. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\sec\\left({\\color{blue}3}x + \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\right) \\geq 1.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the above inequality by ${\\color{red}2}$ and adding ${\\color{green}1},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\operatorname{sec}⁡(3x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\ge 1 \\\\  & 2\\operatorname{sec}⁡(3x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\ge 2 \\\\  & 2\\operatorname{sec}⁡(3x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})+1\\ge 2+1 \\\\  & f(x)\\ge 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x) = 3$ at the local minimum points." 13194139539080 17592186576411 true] [17592186084427 1064 "The secant and cosecant functions have a local minimum on each of the positive branches when the function value is equal to $1,$ as shown below:\n\n![](images/35/35f69692440cd1eb636c591356b0a73d91d30503968764b45e62fe66222e5da9.png)\n\nSimilarly, the functions 