 }.$ This gives ${{\\color{blue}{3}}\\cdot \\color{red}{2}} = {\\color{green}6}.$ Place the result below the next term. |\n| $$\n\\displaystyle {\\begin{array}{rrrrr}\\enclose{bottom right}{3} & 2 & {\\color{magenta}- 5} & - 7 & 11 \\\\  &  & {\\color{green}6} &  &  \\\\ \\hline  & 2 & {\\color{red}1} &  & \\end{array}}\n$$ | Add the last result ($\\color{green}{6}$) to the term located above ($\\color{magenta}{-5}$) and write their sum below the line. This gives ${\\color{magenta}{-5}} + {\\color{green}{6}}={\\color{red}{1}}.$ |\n| $$\n\\displaystyle {\\begin{array}{rrrrr}\\enclose{bottom right}{ {\\color{blue}3}} & 2 & - 5 & - 7 & 11 \\\\  &  & 6 & {\\color{green}3} &  \\\\ \\hline  & 2 & {\\color{red}1} &  & \\end{array}}\n$$ | We now repeat the process. Multiply the last result ($\\color{red}1$ here) by $\\color{blue} 3$ and place the result below the next term. This gives ${\\color{blue}{3}}\\cdot {\\color{red}{1}}= {\\color{green}{3}}.$ |\n| $$\n\\displaystyle {\\begin{array}{rrrrr}\\enclose{bottom right}{3} & 2 & - 5 & {\\color{magenta}- 7} & 11 \\\\  &  & 6 & {\\color{green}3} &  \\\\ \\hline  & 2 & 1 & {\\color{red}- 4} & \\end{array}}\n$$ | Now add the last result ($\\color{green}{3}$) to the term located above ($\\color{magenta}{-7}$) and write their sum below the line. This gives ${\\color{magenta}{-7}} + {\\color{green}{3}}={\\color{red}{-4}}.$ |\n| $$\n\\displaystyle {\\begin{array}{rrrrr}\\enclose{bottom right}{ {\\color{blue}3}} & 2 & - 5 & - 7 & {\\color{magenta}11} \\\\  &  & 6 & 3 & {\\color{green}- 12} \\\\ \\hline  & 2 & 1 & {\\color{red}- 4} & \\boxed{ {\\color{blue}- 1}}\\end{array}}\n$$ | There is one last step. Again, multiply $\\color{blue} 3$ by the last result $\\color{red}-4$ and write it below the constant term. This gives ${\\color{blue}{3}}\\cdot{\\color{red}{(-4)}}={\\color{green}-12}.$ Finally, add $\\color{green}-12$ to $\\color{magenta}11$ to get ${\\color{blue}{-1}},$ which is the remainder. The remainder $\\color{blue}-1$ is important, so we put a box around it. |\n\nThe numbers $2,$ $1$ and $-4$ in the bottom row are the coefficients of the **quotient**. So from left to right, the quotient is\n\n$$\n2x^2+1x-4.\n$$\n\nThe **remainder** is the value in the box, so ${\\color{blue}-1}$ in this case. \n\nFinally, we can write the full result of the division as \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2x^3-5x^2-7x+11}{x-3}=2x^2+x-4+\\dfrac{\\color{blue}-1}{x-3} ,\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2x^3-5x^2-7x+11}{x-3}=2x^2+x-4-\\dfrac{1}{x-3}.\n$$" 13194139539080 17592186576411 true] [17592186084439 1064 "Our goal is to find the quotient and remainder of the following division problem.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2x^3-5x^2-7x+11}{x-3}\n$$\n\nTo do this, we can use **synthetic division**. Synthetic division allows us to divide a polynomial by a factor of the form $(x-c).$ Here's how we do it.\n\n| ${\\begin{array}{rrrrr}\\enclose{bottom right}{ {\\color{blue}3}} & 2 & - 5 & - 7 & 11 \\\\  &  &  &  &  \\\\ \\hline  &  &  &  & \\end{array}}$ | Start off by writing the term $c$ of the divisor on the left side. Here, $c = {\\color{blue}{3}}.$ Then, write the coefficients of all terms of the dividend on the right side in descending order of their degree. Remember the sign! Also, if a term is missing, use $0$ as the coefficient. |\n| --- | --- |\n| ${\\begin{array}{rrrrr}\\enclose{bottom right}{3} & {\\color{red}2} & - 5 & - 7 & 11 \\\\  & ↓ &  &  &  \\\\ \\hline  & {\\color{red}2} &  &  & \\end{array}}$ | Bring down the leading term of the dividend. Here, the leading term is ${\\color{red}2}.$ |\n| ${\\begin{array}{rrrrr}\\enclose{bottom right}{ {\\color{blue}3}} & 2 & - 5 & - 7 & 11 \\\\  &  & {\\color{green}6} &  &  \\\\ \\hline  & {\\color{red}2} &  &  & \\end{array}}$ | Multiply the leading term $\\color{red}2$ by ${\\color{blue}3 }.$ This gives ${{\\color{blue}{3}}\\cdot \\color{red}{2}} = {\\color{green}6}.$ Place the result below the next term. |\n| ${\\begin{array}{rrrrr}\\enclose{bottom right}{3} & 2 & {\\color{magenta}-