t{i})(2+\\text{i})} \\\\  & =\\frac{3(2+\\text{i})}{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}(2+\\text{i}) \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5}+\\frac{3}{5}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn polar form, $r$ represents the magnitude $|z|.$ For a complex number $z=x+y\\textrm{i},$ the magnitude of $z$ is given by\n\n$$\n|z| = \\sqrt{x^2+y^2} .\n$$\n\nSo, for $z= \\dfrac{6}{5} +\\dfrac{3}{5}\\textrm{i},$ the magnitude is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{6}{5}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{36+9}{25}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{45}{25}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn polar form, $\\theta$  represents the argument $\\arg(z).$ Since $x = \\dfrac{6}{5}$ and $y = \\dfrac{3}{5}$ are both positive, $z$ lies in the first quadrant. So, we can find $\\theta$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{6}{5}\\right)}\\right) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =0.464,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places. \n\nFinally, we write the complex number $z$ in polar form:\n\n$$\nz = \\dfrac{3\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\left (\\cos(0.464)+ \\textrm{i} \\sin(0.464) \\right)\n$$" 13194139539080 17592186576411 false] [17592186087530 1071 "Calculate the point where the ellipse $$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{(x-1)^2}{4}+\\dfrac{(y-3)^2}{16}=1\n$$ intercepts the $y$-axis." 13194139539080 17592186576411 true] [17592186087530 1071 "Calculate the point where the ellipse $\\dfrac{(x-1)^2}{4}+\\dfrac{(y-3)^2}{16}=1$ intercepts the $y$-axis." 13194139539080 17592186576411 false] [17592186087533 1071 "For the ellipse $$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{(x-2)^2}{25} + \\dfrac{(y+2)^2}{16} = 1,\n$$ find its center, and the lengths of its major and minor axes." 13194139539080 17592186576411 true] [17592186087533 1071 "For the ellipse $\\dfrac{(x-2)^2}{25} + \\dfrac{(y+2)^2}{16} = 1,$ find its center, and the lengths of its major and minor axes." 13194139539080 17592186576411 false] [17592186087547 1071 "Find the points where the ellipse $$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{(x-2)^2}{4}+\\dfrac{(y-1)^2}{16}=1\n$$ intersects the line $y = -2x + 9$." 13194139539080 17592186576411 true] [17592186087547 1071 "Find the points where the ellipse $\\dfrac{(x-2)^2}{4}+\\dfrac{(y-1)^2}{16}=1$ intersects the line $y = -2x + 9$." 13194139539080 17592186576411 false] [17592186087547 1074 "Let's start by multiplying the equation of the ellipse by $16$ to remove the fractions. This isn't absolutely necessary, but it will make the equation simpler to work with after we substitute in the equation of the line.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n((x - 2)^{2})/(4) + ((y - 1)^{2})/(16) &= 1 \\\\\n16 \\cdot (((x - 2)^{2})/(4) + ((y - 1)^{2})/(16)) &= 16 \\cdot 1 \\\\\n4(x - 2)^{2} + (y - 1)^{2} &= 16\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the points of intersection, we substitute\n\n$$\ny =-2x+9\n$$\n\ninto the equation of the ellipse and solve for $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4(x - 2)^{2} + (y - 1)^{2} &= 16 \\\\\n4(x - 2)^{2} + (-2x + 9 - 1)^{2} &= 16 \\\\\n4(x - 2)^{2} + (-2x + 8)^{2} &= 16 \\\\\n4(x^{2} - 4x + 4) + 4x^{2} - 32x + 64 &= 16 \\\\\n4x^{2} - 16x + 16 + 4x^{2} - 32x + 64 &= 16 \\\\\n8x^{2} - 48x + 64 &= 0 \\\\\nx^{2} - 6x + 8 &= 0 \\\\\n(x - 2)(x - 4) &= 0 \\\\\nx &= 2, 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the corresponding $y$-values, we substitute $x=2,4$ into the equation of the line\n\n$$\ny = -2x + 9\n$$\n\n- Substituting $x=2$, we get\n$$\n\\displaystyle y = -2\\cdot2+9 = 5.\n$$\nThis tells us that the first point of intersection is $\\left(2,5\\right)$.\n- Substituting $x=4$, we get\n$$\n\\displaystyle y = -2\\cdot4+9 = 1.\n$$\nThis tells us that the second point of intersection is $\\left(4,1\\right)$.\n\nTherefore, the intersection points are\n\n$$\n\\left(2,5\\right)\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\left(4,1\\right)\n$$" 13194139539080 17592186576411 true] [17592186087547 1074 "Let's start by multiplying the equation of the ellipse by $16$ to remove the fractions. This isn't absolutely necessary, but it will make the equation simpler to work with after we substitute in the equation of the line.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n((x - 2)^{2})/(4) + ((y - 1)^{2})/(16) &= 1 \\\\\n16 \\cdot (((x - 2)^{2})/(4) + ((y - 1)^{2})/(16)) &= 16 \\cdot 1 \\\\\n4(x - 2)^{2} + (y - 1)^{2} &= 16\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the points of intersection, we substitute\n\n$$\ny =-2x+9\n$$\n\ninto the equation of the ellipse and solve for $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4(x - 2)^{2} + (y - 1)^{2} &= 16 \\\\\n4(x - 2)^{2} + (-2x + 9 - 1)^{2} &= 16 \\\\\n4(x - 2)^{2} + (-2x + 8)^{2} &= 16 \\\\\n4(x^{2} - 4x + 4) + 4x^{2} - 32x + 64 &= 16 \\\\\n4x^{2} - 16x + 16 + 4x^{2} - 32x + 64 &= 16 \\\\\n8x^{2} - 48x + 64 &= 0 \\\\\nx^{2} - 6x + 8 &= 0 \\\\\n(x - 2)(x - 4) &= 0 \\\\\nx &= 2, 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the corresponding $y$-values, we substitute $x=2,4$ into the equation of the line\n\n$$\ny = -2x + 9\n$$\n\n- Substituting $x=2$, we get\n$y = -2\\cdot2+9 = 5$.\nThis tells us that the first point of intersection is $\\left(2,5\\right)$.\n- Substituting $x=4$, we get\n$y = -2\\cdot4+9 = 1$.\nThis tells us that the second point of intersection is $\\left(4,1\\right)$.\n\nTherefore, the intersection points are\n\n$$\n\\left(2,5\\right)\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\left(4,1\\right)\n$$" 13194139539080 17592186576411 false] [17592186087548 1071 "![](images/fd/fdfa82e6fc64c6cfa1040ea76096ee0d68fa65b143f7a35a13bb810aabd0018c.png)\n\nFind the point where the ellipse $$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{(x+3)^2}{6}+\\dfrac{(y-1)^2}{12}=1\n$$ intersects the line $y = -2x+1$, shown above." 13194139539080 17592186576411 true] [17592186087548 1071 "![](images/fd/fdfa82e6fc64c6cfa1040ea76096ee0d68fa65b143f7a35a13bb810aabd0018c.png)\n\nFind the point where the ellipse $\\dfrac{(x+3)^2}{6}+\\dfrac{(y-1)^2}{12}=1$ intersects the line $y = -2x+1$, shown above." 13194139539080 17592186576411 false] [17592186087549 1071 "Find the points where the ellipse $$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{(x-4)^2}{10}+\\dfrac{(y+2)^2}{5} = 1\n$$ intersects the line $y = x - 1$." 13194139539080 17592186576411 true] [17592186087549 1071 "Find the points where the ellipse $\\dfrac{(x-4)^2}{10}+\\dfrac{(y+2)^2}{5} = 1$ intersects the line $y = x - 1$." 13194139539080 17592186576411 false] [17592186087552 1071 "Calculate the $x$-intercepts of the hyperbola $$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{(x+1)^2}{10} - \\dfrac{y^2}{25} = 1.\n$$" 13194139539080 17592186576411 true] [17592186087552 1071 "Calculate the $x$-intercepts of the hyperbola $\\dfrac{(x+1)^2}{10} - \\dfrac{y^2}{25} = 1.$" 13194139539080 17592186576411 false] [17592186087558 1071 "A curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle x=4+t, \\quad y=2-t, \\quad -\\infty < t < \\infty.\n$$\n\nFind the point of intersection of the curve with the $x$-axis." 13194139539080 17592186576411 true] [17592186087558 1071 "A curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$x=4+t, \\quad y=2-t, \\quad -\\infty < t < \\infty$.\n\nFind the point of intersection of the curve with the $x$-axis." 13194139539080 17592186576411 false] [17592186087559 1074 "To find the coordinates of the $x$-intercepts, we set $y=0$ and solve for $t$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= \\sqrt{(t - 2)(3t - 1)} \\\\\n0 &= \\sqrt{(t - 2)(3t - 1)} \\\\\n0 &= (t - 2)(3t - 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side equals zero if one of the factors is zero.\n\n- For the factor $(t-2)$, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle (t - 2)|= 0; t|= 2.\n$$\n- For the factor $(3t-1)$, we get\n$$\n\\displaystyle (3t - 1)|= 0; 3t|= 1; t|= \\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our solutions for $t$ into the equation\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac 1t\n$$\n\n- Substituting $t=2$ gives\n$$\n\\displaystyle x|= \\frac{1}{t}; = \\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n- Substituting $t=\\dfrac{1}{3}$ gives\n$$\n\\displaystyle x|= \\frac{1}{t}; = (1)/(\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)); = 3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-intercepts are\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2},0\\right)\n$$\n\nand $(3,0)$." 13194139539080 17592186576411 true] [17592186087559 1074 "To find the coordinates of the $x$-intercepts, we set $y=0$ and solve for $t$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= \\sqrt{(t - 2)(3t - 1)} \\\\\n0 &= \\sqrt{(t - 2)(3t - 1)} \\\\\n0 &= (t - 2)(3t - 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side equals zero if one of the factors is zero.\n\n- For the factor $(t-2)$, we have\n$(t - 2)|= 0; t|= 2$.\n- For the factor $(3t-1)$, we get\n$(3t - 1)|= 0; 3t|= 1; t|= \\frac{1}{3}$.\n\nThen, we substitute our solutions for $t$ into the equation\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac 1t\n$$\n\n- Substituting $t=2$ gives\n$x|= \\frac{1}{t}; = \\frac{1}{2}$.\n- Substituting $t=\\dfrac{1}{3}$ gives\n$x|= \\frac{1}{t}; = (1)/(\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)); = 3$.\n\nTherefore, the $x$-intercepts are\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2},0\\right)\n$$\n\nand $(3,0)$." 13194139539080 17592186576411 false] [17592186087560 1071 "A curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle x=t-3,\\quad y=4-t^2, \\quad -\\infty < t < \\infty\n$$\n\nFind its point of intersection with the $y$-axis." 13194139539080 17592186576411 true] [17592186087560 1071 "A curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$x=t-3,\\quad y=4-t^2, \\quad -\\infty < t < \\infty$\n\nFind its point of intersection with the $y$-axis." 13194139539080 17592186576411 false] [17592186087602 1074 "**Step 1:** First we find the complementary solution $y_c$ by solving the homogeneous ODE. The characteristic equation\n\n$$\n\\lambda^2 + \\lambda - 2 = 0\n$$\n\nhas roots $\\lambda = 1,-2,$ so the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c = Ae^{x} + Be^{- 2 x}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Second, we find a particular solution $y_p$ of the inhomogeneous ODE. Since the right hand side is a polynomial of degree $2,$ a particular solution will also be a polynomial of degree $2.$ So we assume\n\n$$\ny_p =\\alpha x^2 + \\beta x + \\gamma\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha,$ $\\beta,$ and $\\gamma$ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of $y_p,$ we get\n\n$$\ny_p' = 2\\alpha x\n+\\beta,\\qquad y_p'' = 2\\alpha.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha,$ $\\beta,$ and $\\gamma,$ we substitute the derivatives into the original ODE and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\alpha +(2\\alpha x+\\beta )-2(\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma ) & ={x}^{2}+5x-3 \\\\ -2\\alpha {x}^{2}+(2\\alpha -2\\beta )x+(2\\alpha +\\beta -2\\gamma ) & ={x}^{2}+5x-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-2\\alpha &=1\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,{x}^{2}) \\\\ 2\\alpha -2\\beta &=5\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ 2\\alpha +\\beta -2\\gamma &=-3\\, && (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $$\n\\displaystyle \\alpha=-\\dfrac{1}{2}, \\beta=-3, \\gamma=-\\dfrac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\ny_p =- \\frac{x^2}{2} - 3 x - \\dfrac 1 2.\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** We write the general solution as the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={y}_{c}+{y}_{p} \\\\  & =A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{-2x}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-3x-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139539080 17592186576411 true] [17592186087602 1074 "**Step 1:** First we find the complementary solution $y_c$ by solving the homogeneous ODE. The characteristic equation\n\n$$\n\\lambda^2 + \\lambda - 2 = 0\n$$\n\nhas roots $\\lambda = 1,-2,$ so the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c = Ae^{x} + Be^{- 2 x}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Second, we find a particular solution $y_p$ of the inhomogeneous ODE. Since the right hand side is a polynomial of degree $2,$ a particular solution will also be a polynomial of degree $2.$ So we assume\n\n$$\ny_p =\\alpha x^2 + \\beta x + \\gamma\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha,$ $\\beta,$ and $\\gamma$ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of $y_p,$ we get\n\n$$\ny_p' = 2\\alpha x\n+\\beta,\\qquad y_p'' = 2\\alpha.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha,$ $\\beta,$ and $\\gamma,$ we substitute the derivatives into the original ODE and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\alpha +(2\\alpha x+\\beta )-2(\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma ) & ={x}^{2}+5x-3 \\\\ -2\\alpha {x}^{2}+(2\\alpha -2\\beta )x+(2\\alpha +\\beta -2\\gamma ) & ={x}^{2}+5x-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-2\\alpha &=1\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,{x}^{2}) \\\\ 2\\alpha -2\\beta &=5\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ 2\\alpha +\\beta -2\\gamma &=-3\\, && (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha=-\\dfrac{1}{2}, \\beta=-3, \\gamma=-\\dfrac{1}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\ny_p =- \\frac{x^2}{2} - 3 x - \\dfrac 1 2.\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** We write the general solution as the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={y}_{c}+{y}_{p} \\\\  & =A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{-2x}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-3x-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139539080 17592186576411 false] [17592186087617 1071 "Determine whether the series below converges or diverges.\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac 2 {7n^5}.\n$$" 13194139539080 17592186576411 true] [17592186087617 1071 "Determine whether the series below converges or diverges.\n$\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac 2 {7n^5}$." 13194139539080 17592186576411 false] [17592186087618 1071 "Determine whether the series below converges or diverges.\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac 1 {\\sqrt {n^5}}.\n$$" 13194139539080 17592186576411 true] [17592186087618 1071 "Determine whether the series below converges or diverges.\n$\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac 1 {\\sqrt {n^5}}$." 13194139539080 17592186576411 false] [17592186087619 1071 "Which of the following series are convergent?\n\n1. $$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{n^2\\sqrt n}\n$$\n2. $$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{5}{n^2}\n$$\n3. $$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{\\sqrt{n}}{\\sqrt{n^3}}\n$$" 13194139539080 17592186576411 true] [17592186087619 1071 "Which of the following series are convergent?\n\n1. $\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{n^2\\sqrt n}$\n2. $\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{5}{n^2}$\n3. $\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{\\sqrt{n}}{\\sqrt{n^3}}$" 13194139539080 17592186576411 false] [17592186087619 1074 "We analyze each series in turn.\n\n- First, we look at $$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{n^2\\sqrt n}.\n$$ Rewriting this series gives\n$$\n\\displaystyle ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)\\frac{1}{n^{2}\\sqrt{n}}|= ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)\\frac{1}{n^{2} \\cdot n^{1/2}}; = ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)\\frac{1}{n^{5/2}},\n$$\nwhich is a $p$-series with $p=\\dfrac 5 2$. Since $p > 1$, the series converges by the $p$-series test.\n- For the second series, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{5}{n^2} = 5\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{{n^2}},\n$$\nwhich is a $p$-series with $p=2$. Since $p>1$, the series is convergent by the $p$-series test.\n- For the third series, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)\\frac{\\sqrt{n}}{\\sqrt{n^{3}}}|= ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)\\frac{n^{1/2}}{n^{3/2}}; = ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)\\frac{1}{n},\n$$\nwhich is a $p$-series with $p=1$ (the harmonic series). Since $p \\leq 1$, the series diverges by the $p$-series test.\n\nTherefore, only series I and II are convergent." 13194139539080 17592186576411 true] [17592186087619 1074 "We analyze each series in turn.\n\n- First, we look at $\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{n^2\\sqrt n}$. Rewriting this series gives\n$∑_(n = 1)^(∞)\\frac{1}{n^{2}\\sqrt{n}}|= ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)\\frac{1}{n^{2} \\cdot n^{1/2}}; = ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)\\frac{1}{n^{5/2}}$,\nwhich is a $p$-series with $p=\\dfrac 5 2$. Since $p > 1$, the series converges by the $p$-series test.\n- For the second series, 