e 4$,\nconsider the following table of values:\n\n| **$t$** | $-4$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $x$ | $12$ | $a$ | $0$ | $0$ | $2$ | $c$ | $20$ |\n| $y$ | $20$ | $6$ | $2$ | $b$ | $0$ | $6$ | $12$ |\n\nFind the value of $a + b + c$." 13194139539080 17592186576411 false] [17592186088280 1074 "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is ${x}^{3}.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by ${x}^{3},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2x+1}{9x+8{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{2x}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{9x}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{8{x}^{2}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{9}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{8}{x}-1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{2}{{x}^{2}},\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}},\\frac{9}{{x}^{2}}\n$$ and $\\frac{8}{x}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{9}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{8}{x}-1)} \\\\  & \\to \\frac{0+0}{0+0-1} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the horizontal asymptote is $y=0.$" 13194139539080 17592186576411 true] [17592186088280 1074 "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is ${x}^{3}.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by ${x}^{3},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2x+1}{9x+8{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{2x}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{9x}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{8{x}^{2}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{9}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{8}{x}-1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}},\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}},\\frac{9}{{x}^{2}}$ and $\\frac{8}{x}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{9}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{8}{x}-1)} \\\\  & \\to \\frac{0+0}{0+0-1} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the horizontal asymptote is $y=0.$" 13194139539080 17592186576411 false] [17592186088281 1071 "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n$$\n\\displaystyle x = 3\\cos θ - 1, y = 2\\sin θ + 2, 0 \\le θ < 2π,\n$$\nwhat are the ranges of $x$ and $y$?" 13194139539080 17592186576411 true] [17592186088281 1071 "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n$x = 3\\cos θ - 1, y = 2\\sin θ + 2, 0 \\le θ < 2π$,\nwhat are the ranges of $x$ and $y$?" 13194139539080 17592186576411 false] [17592186088346 1071 "Find the general solution to the equation\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} + 6\\frac{dy}{dx} + 9y = 0.\n$$" 13194139539080 17592186576411 true] [17592186088346 1071 "Find the general solution to the equation\n$\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} + 6\\frac{dy}{dx} + 9y = 0$." 13194139539080 17592186576411 false] [17592186088352 1071 "Find the general solution to the equation\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} - 4\\frac{dy}{dx} + 13y = 0.\n$$" 13194139539080 17592186576411 true] [17592186088352 1071 "Find the general solution to the equation\n$\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} - 4\\frac{dy}{dx} + 13y = 0$." 13194139539080 17592186576411 false] [17592186088366 1071 "Consider the matrices\n$$\n\\displaystyle A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}, B = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}, C = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $AB = BA = I_{2}$\n2. $AC = CA = I_{