eft(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)) \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+\\text{i}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =3+3\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis matches up with our original complex number, so we can be sure that we have the correct polar form." 13194139539080 17592186576411 false] [17592186088922 1064 "Consider the ellipse\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{12} + \\dfrac{y^2}{24} = 1\n$$\n\nand line $y = 2x$, sketched below. We can see from the diagram that the line and the ellipse intersect at $(2,4)$ and $(-2,-4)$.\n\n![](images/b0/b0a3676e298879fb74c0488ded0ad6fd469592eeba21f4d96dc8e5555886b641.png)\n\nBut how can we determine this mathematically, without relying on the graph?\n\nTo find the $x$-coordinates of the intersection points, we can substitute $y = 2x$ into the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{x^{2}}{12} + \\frac{y^{2}}{24} &= 1 \\\\\n\\frac{x^{2}}{12} + ((2x)^{2})/(24) &= 1 \\\\\n\\frac{x^{2}}{12} + \\frac{4x^{2}}{24} &= 1 \\\\\n2x^{2} + 4x^{2} &= 24 \\\\\n6x^{2} &= 24 \\\\\nx^{2} &= 4 \\\\\nx &= ± 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, to find the $y$-coordinates of the intersection points, we can substitute $x = \\pm 2$ into the equation of the line and compute the $y$-values.\n\n- Substituting $x = -2$, we get\n$$\n\\displaystyle y|= 2x; y|= 2 \\cdot (-2); y|=-4.\n$$\nThis tells us that one intersection point is $(-2, -4)$.\n- Substituting $x = 2$, we get\n$$\n\\displaystyle y|= 2x; y|= 2 \\cdot 2; y|= 4.\n$$\nThis tells us that the other intersection point is $(2, 4)$.\n\nSo we find that the two intersection points are $(-2,-4)$ and $(2,4)$, which matches up perfectly with what we see in the graph." 13194139539080 17592186576411 true] [17592186088922 1064 "Consider the ellipse\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{12} + \\dfrac{y^2}{24} = 1\n$$\n\nand line $y = 2x$, sketched below. We can see from the diagram that the line and the ellipse intersect at $(2,4)$ and $(-2,-4)$.\n\n![](images/b0/b0a3676e298879fb74c0488ded0ad6fd469592eeba21f4d96dc8e5555886b641.png)\n\nBut how can we determine this mathematically, without relying on the graph?\n\nTo find the $x$-coordinates of the intersection points, we can substitute $y = 2x$ into the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{x^{2}}{12} + \\frac{y^{2}}{24} &= 1 \\\\\n\\frac{x^{2}}{12} + ((2x)^{2})/(24) &= 1 \\\\\n\\frac{x^{2}}{12} + \\frac{4x^{2}}{24} &= 1 \\\\\n2x^{2} + 4x^{2} &= 24 \\\\\n6x^{2} &= 24 \\\\\nx^{2} &= 4 \\\\\nx &= ± 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, to find the $y$-coordinates of the intersection points, we can substitute $x = \\pm 2$ into the equation of the line and compute the $y$-values.\n\n- Substituting $x = -2$, we get\n$y|= 2x; y|= 2 \\cdot (-2); y|=-4$.\nThis tells us that one intersection point is $(-2, -4)$.\n- Substituting $x = 2$, we get\n$y|= 2x; y|= 2 \\cdot 2; y|= 4$.\nThis tells us that the other intersection point is $(2, 4)$.\n\nSo we find that the two intersection points are $(-2,-4)$ and $(2,4)$, which matches up perfectly with what we see in the graph." 13194139539080 17592186576411 false] [17592186088928 1064 "Consider the function $f(x),$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x) = 2x+1.\n$$\n\nThis function $f$ takes a real number $x$ as input, and it outputs a real number. We can describe this fact using the following notation:\n\n$$\nf: x \\longrightarrow 2x+1\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we can define a function $\\mathbf T,$ also called a **transformation** or a **map**, that takes a 2D-vector (or 3D-vector) as input and outputs a new 2D-vector (or, respectively, 3D-vector). \n\nFor example, let's consider the following transformation: \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis means that for any 2D-vector $\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}$ the corresponding image $\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}$ can be found as follows:\n\