7592186093360 1064 "Consider the polar curves $r=2(1+\\cos\\theta)$ and $r=2.$ What is the area of the shaded region shown below?\n\n![](images/c0/c011bf3b40639e10ec151541d0892d5095b2d983b6ed03247824f06f225c10d9.png)\n\nFirst, we find the values of $\\theta$ for which the two curves intersect:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) & =2 \\\\ 1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us two solutions for $$\n\\displaystyle \\theta \\in \\left[-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2},\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right],\n$$ namely\n\n$$\n\\theta = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\nTo find the area, we use the formula\n\n$$\nA = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2} \\Big( \\big[ {\\color{brown}\\text{outer curve}} \\big]^2 - \\big[ {\\color{blue}\\text{inner curve}} \\big]^2 \\Big) \\:\\textrm{d}\\theta,\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$\\qquad$ $\\theta_1=-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\quad$ and $\\quad \\theta_2=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}.$ \n\nTo determine the inner function and the outer, let's draw an arrow from the origin that passes through the shaded region.\n\n![](images/7b/7b77af383d0d05d00c91db364e17675dfd45445eb5ac8e2926f8bb90220bc0fe.png)\n\nThe arrow\n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $r=2,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer function $r=2(1+\\cos\\theta).$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}({(2(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ))}^{2}-{2}^{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(4(1+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )-4)\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139539080 17592186576411 true] [17592186093360 1064 "Consider the polar curves $r=2(1+\\cos\\theta)$ and $r=2.$ What is the area of the shaded region shown below?\n\n![](images/c0/c011bf3b40639e10ec151541d0892d5095b2d983b6ed03247824f06f225c10d9.png)\n\nFirst, we find the values of $\\theta$ for which the two curves intersect:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) & =2 \\\\ 1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us two solutions for $\\theta \\in \\left[-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2},\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right],$ namely\n\n$$\n\\theta = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\nTo find the area, we use the formula\n\n$$\nA = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2} \\Big( \\big[ {\\color{brown}\\text{outer curve}} \\big]^2 - \\big[ {\\color{blue}\\text{inner curve}} \\big]^2 \\Big) \\:\\textrm{d}\\theta,\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$\\qquad$ $\\theta_1=-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\quad$ and $\\quad \\theta_2=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}.$ \n\nTo determine the inner function and the outer, let's draw an arrow from the origin that passes through the shaded region.\n\n![](images/7b/7b77af383d0d05d00c91db364e17675dfd45445eb5ac8e2926f8bb90220bc0fe.png)\n\nThe arrow\n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $r=2,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer function $r=2(1+\\cos\\theta).$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}({(2(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ))}^{2}-{2}^{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(4(1+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )-4)\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139539080 17592186576411 false] [17592186093361 1064 "To calculate the arc length of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta)$ between the rays $\\theta=\\theta_1$ and $\\theta=\\theta_2,$ we can use the formula\n\n$$\nL = \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2} \\sqrt{r^2 +\\left(\\frac{\\textrm{d}r}{\\textrm{d}\\theta}\\right)^2 }\\textrm{d}\\theta.\n$$\n\nFor example, suppose that we want to find the arc length of the polar curve $r = 2,$ where $$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{\\pi}{6} \\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}.\n$$ The required arc length is shown in blue in the picture below.\n\n![](images/e9/e91cd717469db56426e39e8975aa04710ff2357c7a0d5de9d20f99bc5edcbb1c.png)\n\nWe will use the formula to compute the arc length. Since $r=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}r}{\\textrm{d}\\theta} = 0,\n$$\n\nand therefore, the arc length can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{2\\pi /3}\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{(0)}^{2}}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{2\\pi /3}2\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{2\\pi /3}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\theta \\,{|}_{\\pi /6}^{2\\pi /3} \\\\  & =2(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139539080 17592186576411 true] [17592186093361 1064 "To calculate the arc length of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta)$ between the rays $\\theta=\\theta_1$ and $\\theta=\\theta_2,$ we can use the formula\n\n$$\nL = \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2} \\sqrt{r^2 +\\left(\\frac{\\textrm{d}r}{\\textrm{d}\\theta}\\right)^2 }\\textrm{d}\\theta.\n$$\n\nFor example, suppose that we want to find the arc length of the polar curve $r = 2,$ where $\\dfrac{\\pi}{6} \\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}.$ The required arc length is shown in blue in the picture below.\n\n![](images/e9/e91cd717469db56426e39e8975aa04710ff2357c7a0d5de9d20f99bc5edcbb1c.png)\n\nWe will use the formula to compute the arc length. Since $r=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}r}{\\textrm{d}\\theta} = 0,\n$$\n\nand therefore, the arc length can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{2\\pi /3}\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{(0)}^{2}}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{2\\pi /3}2\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{2\\pi /3}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\theta \\,{|}_{\\pi /6}^{2\\pi /3} \\\\  & =2(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139539080 17592186576411 false] [17592186093367 1064 "Consider the infinite series $$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty  \\frac{1}{5^n}.\n$$ The first five partial sums of this series are:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}_{1} & =0.2 \\\\ {s}_{2} & =0.24 \\\\ {s}_{3} & =0.248 \\\\ {s}_{4} & =0.2496 \\\\ {s}_{5} & =0.24992\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's plot these values on a graph of $s_n$ versus $n.$\n\n![](images/98/9892c2dc01bc364280ec281ef543a88ec1fcdfa03bf2d5ad998befde2dd37fc5.png)\n\nLooking at the graph above, it appears as though\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to\\infty} s_n = 0.25.\n$$\n\nAlthough we haven't shown it rigorously, the above statement is indeed true. We say that **the limit of the sequence of partial sums** is equal to $0.25,$ and we write\n\n$$\n\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty  \\frac{1}{5^n} = 0.25.\n$$\n\nIn general, if the limit of the partial sums of a series $$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n\n$$ is some finite number $S,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to\\infty} s_n = S\n$$\n\nthen we say that the infinite series is **convergent** and that it **converges** to $S.$ We also write\n\n$$\n\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n = S.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, if the limit does not exist or is infinite, we say that the series is **divergent**." 13194139539080 17592186576411 true] [17592186093367 1064 "Consider the infinite series $\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty  \\frac{1}{5^n}.$ The first five partial sums of this series are:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}_{1} & =0.2 \\\\ {s}_{2} & =0.24 \\\\ {s}_{3} & =0.248 \\\\ {s}_{4} & =0.2496 \\\\ {s}_{5} & =0.24992\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's plot these values on a graph of $s_n$ versus $n.$\n\n![](images/98/9892c2dc01bc364280ec281ef543a88ec1fcdfa03bf2d5ad998befde2dd37fc5.png)\n\nLooking at the graph above, it appears as though\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to\\infty} s_n = 0.25.\n$$\n\nAlthough we haven't shown it rigorously, the above statement is indeed true. We say that **the limit of the sequence of partial sums** is equal to $0.25,$ and we write\n\n$$\n\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty  \\frac{1}{5^n} = 0.25.\n$$\n\nIn general, if the limit of the partial sums of a series $\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n$ is some finite number $S,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to\\infty} s_n = S\n$$\n\nthen we say that the infinite series is **convergent** and that it **converges** to $S.$ We also write\n\n$$\n\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n = S.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, if the limit does not exist or is infinite, we say that the series is **divergent**." 13194139539080 17592186576411 false] [17592186093479 1064 "Note the following rules regarding the multiplication of an infinite series by a scalar $K\\mathbin{:}$\n\n- If $$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n\n$$ is convergent, then $$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty K\\cdot a_n = K\\cdot \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n\n$$ is convergent for any real number $K.$\n\n- If $$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n\n$$ is divergent, then $$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty K\\cdot a_n\n$$ is divergent for any real number $K\\neq 0.$" 13194139539080 17592186576411 true] [17592186093479 1064 "Note the following rules regarding the multiplication of an infinite series by a scalar $K\\mathbin{:}$\n\n- If $\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n$ is convergent, then $\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty K\\cdot a_n = K\\cdot \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n$ is convergent for any real number $K.$\n\n- If $\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n$ is divergent, then $\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty K\\cdot a_n$ is divergent for any real number $K\\neq 0.$" 13194139539080 17592186576411 false] [17592186096367 1074 "Suppose the sequence $a_n$ is defined for\n\n$$\nn\\geq 1\n$$\n\n- $a_n$ is **increasing** if every term is greater than or equal to the previous term.\n- $a_n$ is **decreasing** if every term is less than or equal to the previous term.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each sequence in turn.\n\n- The sequence $a_n$ is decreasing. Computing the first few terms, we have\n$-2, \\quad -4, \\quad -8, \\quad -16, \\quad \\ldots$\nFrom here, we see that every term is less than the previous term.\n- The sequence $b_n$ is *increasing*. Computing the first few terms, we have\n$1, \\quad 2, \\quad 6, \\quad 24, \\quad \\ldots$\nFrom here, we see that every term is greater than the previous term.\n- The sequence $c_n$ is decreasing. Computing the first few terms, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{2}{\\sqrt[3]1}, \\quad \\dfrac{2}{\\sqrt[3]2}, \\quad \\dfrac{2}{\\sqrt[3]3}, \\quad \\dfrac{2}{\\sqrt[3]4}, \\quad \\ldots\n$$\nFrom here, we see that as $n$ increases, the numerator remains constant while the denominator increases. So every term is less than the previous term.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$a_n$ and $c_n$ only.\"" 13194139539080 17592186576411 true] [17592186096367 1074 "Suppose the sequence $a_n$ is defined for\n\n$$\nn\\geq 1\n$$\n\n- $a_n$ is **increasing** if every term is greater than or equal to the previous term.\n- $a_n$ is **decreasing** if every term is less than or equal to the previous term.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each sequence in turn.\n\n- The sequence $a_n$ is decreasing. Computing the first few terms, we have\n$-2, \\quad -4, \\quad -8, \\quad -16, \\quad \\ldots$\nFrom here, we see that every term is less than the previous term.\n- The sequence $b_n$ is *increasing*. Computing the first few terms, we have\n$1, \\quad 2, \\quad 6, \\quad 24, \\quad \\ldots$\nFrom here, we see that every term is greater than the previous term.\n- The sequence $c_n$ is decreasing. Computing the first few terms, we have\n$\\dfrac{2}{\\sqrt[3]1}, \\quad \\dfrac{2}{\\sqrt[3]2}, \\quad \\dfrac{2}{\\sqrt[3]3}, \\quad \\dfrac{2}{\\sqrt[3]4}, \\quad \\ldots$\nFrom here, we see that as $n$ increases, the numerator remains constant while the denominator increases. So every term is less than the previous term.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$a_n$ and $c_n$ only.\"" 13194139539080 17592186576411 false] [17592186096405 1074 "We want to find the best model for the scatterplot data shown, in the quadratic form\n\n$$\ny = ax^2+bx+c\n$$\n\nwhere $a$, $b$, and $c$ are constants, with\n\n$$\na \\neq 0\n$$\n\n- The data in the scatterplot appear to form a downward-opening parabola. Therefore, the leading coefficient $a$ must be negative.\n- The points look symmetric about the line $x=4$. Using the axis of symmetry formula $x=-\\dfrac{b}{2a}$ and solving for $b$, we get\n$$\n\\displaystyle x|=-\\frac{b}{2a}; 4|=-\\frac{b}{2a}; b|=-8a.\n$$\nSince $a$ is negative, the coefficient of the linear term should be positive.\n- The $y$-intercept of the quadratic model is near $(0,650)$.\n\nTherefore, of the given choices, only choice\n\n$$\ny = -2.28x^{2} + 20.2x+652.76\n$$\n\nis the best quadratic model for the data in the scatterplot." 13194139539080 17592186576411 true] [17592186096405 1074 "We want to find the best model for the scatterplot data shown, in the quadratic form\n\n$$\ny = ax^2+bx+c\n$$\n\nwhere $a$, $b$, and $c$ are constants, with\n\n$$\na \\neq 0\n$$\n\n- The data in the scatterplot appear to form a downward-opening parabola. Therefore, the leading coefficient $a$ must be negative.\n- The points look symmetric about the line $x=4$. Using the axis of symmetry formula $x=-\\dfrac{b}{2a}$ and solving for $b$, we get\n$x|=-\\frac{b}{2a}; 4|=-\\frac{b}{2a}; b|=-8a$.\nSince $a$ is negative, the coefficient of the linear term should be positive.\n- The $y$-intercept of the quadratic model is near $(0,650)$.\n\nTherefore, of the given choices, only choice\n\n$$\ny = -2.28x^{2} + 20.2x+652.76\n$$\n\nis the best quadratic model for the data in the scatterplot." 13194139539080 17592186576411 false] [17592186096427 1071 "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\ln{x}\\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$" 13194139539080 175921865