666bed1891445bc.png)\n\nRecall that a general parabola has the equation\n\n$$\ny = ax^2 + bx + c\n$$\n\nLet's now examine our options in turn.\n\n- We have a downward parabola. So, $a < 0$. As a result, we can reject the following options:\n$y = 2.1x^2 + 0.7x + 1, \\qquad y = 2.1x^2 - 0.7x - 1$\n- The $y$-intercept of our parabola is positive. So, we must have $c \\gt 0$. As a result, we also reject the following options:\n$y = -2.1x^2 + 0.7x - 1$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is\n\n$$\ny = -2.1x^2 + 0.7x + 1\n$$" 13194139539080 17592186576411 false] [17592186096539 1074 "Let's inspect each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since there were $25$ total students, and only $5$ of them passed both tests, we have that\n$$\n\\displaystyle P(A)=\\dfrac{5}{25}=0.2.\n$$\n- Statement II is true. The complement $A'$ is the opposite of the event $A$, \"the student passed both tests.\" So $A'$ is the event \"the student failed at least one test.\"\n- Statement III is true. Since we know that $P(A) = 0.2$, we can calculate $P\\left(A'\\right)$ using the formula\n$$\n\\displaystyle P(A) + P(A^{′})|= 1; P(A^{′})|= 1 - P(A); = 1 - 0.2; = 0.8.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139539080 17592186576411 true] [17592186096539 1074 "Let's inspect each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since there were $25$ total students, and only $5$ of them passed both tests, we have that\n$P(A)=\\dfrac{5}{25}=0.2$.\n- Statement II is true. The complement $A'$ is the opposite of the event $A$, \"the student passed both tests.\" So $A'$ is the event \"the student failed at least one test.\"\n- Statement III is true. Since we know that $P(A) = 0.2$, we can calculate $P\\left(A'\\right)$ using the formula\n$P(A) + P(A^{′})|= 1; P(A^{′})|= 1 - P(A); = 1 - 0.2; = 0.8$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139539080 17592186576411 false] [17592186096543 1071 "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\left\\langle\\cos(2t+1), \\, 4\\right\\rangle \\,\\textrm d t\\,.\n$$" 13194139539080 17592186576411 true] [17592186096543 1071 "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int \\left\\langle\\cos(2t+1), \\, 4\\right\\rangle \\,\\textrm d t\\,.$" 13194139539080 17592186576411 false] [17592186096544 1071 "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_0^{2} \\left\\langle 2e^{t},\\, 6t^2\\right\\rangle\\, \\textrm d t.\n$$" 13194139539080 17592186576411 true] [17592186096544 1071 "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_0^{2} \\left\\langle 2e^{t},\\, 6t^2\\right\\rangle\\, \\textrm d t.$" 13194139539080 17592186576411 false] [17592186096555 1074 "First, we make $x$ the subject:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt{x+1} \\\\ {y}^{2} & =x+1 \\\\ x & ={y}^{2}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the area, we compute the corresponding definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({y}^{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}|}_{0}^{1}-y{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{3}-0)-(1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}-1 \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe obtained a negative number because the region lies to the left of the $y$-axis. So the area that we want is $$\n\\displaystyle \\left|-\\dfrac{2}{3}\\right| = \\dfrac{2}{3}.\n$$" 13194139539080 17592186576411 true] [17592186096555 1074 "First, we make $x$ the subject:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt{x+1} \\\\ {y}^{2} & =x+1 \\\\ x & ={y}^{2}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the area, we compute the corresponding definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({y}^{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}|}_{0}^{1}-y{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{3}-0)-(1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}-1 \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe obtained a negative number because the region lies to the left of the $y$-axis. So the area that we want is $\\left|-\\dfrac{2}{3}\\right| = \\dfrac{2}{3}.$" 13194139539080 17592186576411 false] [17592186096557 1074 "Suppose the sequence $a_n$ is defined for\n\n$$\nn\\geq 1\n$$\n\nTo determine whether $a_n$ is increasing or decreasing, we can use the following test:\n\n- If a sequence $a_n$ is *increasing,* then for all $n\\geq 1$, we have\n$a_{n+1} \\geq a_{n} \\qquad \\Longrightarrow\\qquad a_{n+1} - a_n \\geq 0$.\n- If a sequence $a_n$ is *decreasing,* then for all $n\\geq 1$, we have\n$a_{n+1} \\leq a_{n} \\qquad \\Longrightarrow\\qquad a_{n+1} - a_n \\leq 0$.\n- If neither of these conditions holds, the sequence is *neither increasing nor decreasing*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the difference $a_{n+1} - a_n$, we get\n$$\n\\displaystyle a_{n + 1} - a_{n}|= 3 - 2(n + 1) - (n + 1)^{2} - (3 - 2n - n^{2}); = 3 - 2n - 2 - (n^{2} + 2n + 1) - 3 + 2n + n^{2}; = 3 - 2n - 2 - n^{2} - 2n - 1 - 3 + 2n + n^{2}; =-3 - 2n\n$$\nwhich is *negative* for all $n\\geq 1$. To see this concretely, set $b_n = -3-2n$. Computing the first few terms of $b_n$ we get\n$-5, \\quad -7, \\quad -9, \\quad -11, \\quad \\ldots\\,$.\n\n- Statement II is false, whereas statement III is true. Since $a_{n+1} - a_n \\leq 0$ for all $n\\geq 1$, we can conclude that $a_n$ is a decreasing sequence.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139539080 17592186576411 true] [17592186096557 1074 "Suppose the sequence $a_n$ is defined for\n\n$$\nn\\geq 1\n$$\n\nTo determine whether $a_n$ is increasing or decreasing, we can use the following test:\n\n- If a sequence $a_n$ is *increasing,* then for all $n\\geq 1$, we have\n$a_{n+1} \\geq a_{n} \\qquad \\Longrightarrow\\qquad a_{n+1} - a_n \\geq 0$.\n- If a sequence $a_n$ is *decreasing,* then for all $n\\geq 1$, we have\n$a_{n+1} \\leq a_{n} \\qquad \\Longrightarrow\\qquad a_{n+1} - a_n \\leq 0$.\n- If neither of these conditions holds, the sequence is *neither increasing nor decreasing*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the difference $a_{n+1} - a_n$, we get\n$a_{n + 1} - a_{n}|= 3 - 2(n + 1) - (n + 1)^{2} - (3 - 2n - n^{2}); = 3 - 2n - 2 - (n^{2} + 2n + 1) - 3 + 2n + n^{2}; = 3 - 2n - 2 - n^{2} - 2n - 1 - 3 + 2n + n^{2}; =-3 - 2n$\nwhich is *negative* for all $n\\geq 1$. To see this concretely, set $b_n = -3-2n$. Computing the first few terms of $b_n$ we get\n$-5, \\quad -7, \\quad -9, \\quad -11, \\quad \\ldots\\,$.\n\n- Statement II is false, whereas statement III is true. Since $a_{n+1} - a_n \\leq 0$ for all $n\\geq 1$, we can conclude that $a_n$ is a decreasing sequence.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139539080 17592186576411 false] [17592186096600 1074 "The total surface area $(S)$ of a pyramid equals the sum of the lateral surface area $(S_L)$ and the base area $(S_B)$ of the pyramid:\n\n$$\nS = S_L + S_B\n$$\n\nWith this in mind, let's find $S_L$ and $S_B$ in turn.\n\n- We can find the lateral surface area of a regular pyramid using the formula\n$S_L = \\dfrac{1}{2} p l$,\nwhere $p$ is the base's perimeter and $l$ is the pyramid's slant height.Given the side $s = 7 \\, \\textrm{cm}$ of the regular (equilateral) triangular base, its perimeter is equal to\n$$\n\\displaystyle p|= 3s = 3(7) = 21yd.\n$$\nNow, substituting $p = 21 \\, \\textrm{yd}$ and $l = 12 \\, \\textrm{yd}$ into the formula, we get\n$$\n\\displaystyle S_L = \\dfrac{1}{2}(21)(12) = 126 \\, \\textrm{yd}^2.\n$$\n- The base of the triangular pyramid is an equilateral triangle. In our cases, it's a triangle with side length $s = 7 \\, \\textrm{yd}$. So, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle S_B = \\dfrac{s^2\\sqrt{3}}{4} = \\dfrac{7^2\\sqrt{3}}{4} = \\dfrac{49\\sqrt{3}}{4}\\, \\textrm{yd}^2.\n$$\n\nFinally, the total surface area of the pyramid is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS &= S_{L} + S_{B} \\\\\n&= (126 + \\frac{49\\sqrt{3}}{4})yd^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139539080 17592186576411 true] [17592186096600 1074 "The total surface area $(S)$ of a 